          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              In order to express this kinship of these different cohomological theories, I formulated the notion of “motive” associated to an algebraic variety. By this term I want to suggest that it is the “common motive” (or “common reason”) behind this multitude of cohomological invariants attached to an algebraic variety, or indeed, behind all cohomological invariants that are a priori possible. (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"}> Grothendieck
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Récoltes et Semailles
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/R%C3%A9coltes+et+Semailles"} ?
          %p
            The similarity of the behaviour of various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomologies"} cohomologies
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} varieties
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            suggests that there is a universal one among them with values in an intermediate
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                of motives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is associated a motive
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            factors through the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            One distinguishes a theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motives"} pure motives
            for smooth projective varieties from a more general theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+motives"} mixed motives
            for arbitrary smooth varieties. So far, pure motives and mixed motives have only been defined conditionally. However there are several equivalent definitions of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
            which has all conjectured structural properties of the derived category of mixed motives (except for the t-structure which would make it a derived category).
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"}> Grothendieck
            ‘s original realization of this idea is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+Chow+motives"} category of Chow motives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+spans"} category of spans
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+variety"} smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+varieties"} projective varieties
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy-theoretic
            version was given, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Voevodsky+motives"} Voevodsky motives
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em derived motives
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DerivedMotives"} below
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
            obtained by passing to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topology
            the role of the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
            is played by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
          %p
            Constructions of motives often depend on whether we work in prime
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            than varieties.
          %p
            Another crucial idea leading to motives is that the various cohomology theories lead to the same pieces of information; therefore there is a symmetry related to this, which is of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            nature. For example, over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            one can compare the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Betti+cohomology"} Betti cohomology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            “realizations”. Thus one has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
            structure which is also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
            of motives.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannakian+reconstruction"} Tannakian reconstruction
            plays a major role; for pure motives we have neutral
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannakian+category"} Tannakian categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            of the isomorphism between “realizations” correspond to the matrices of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/period"} period
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            \-functions (and
            = succeed "-functions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ζ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+program"} Langlands program
            indirectly involves motives; in particular its essential part can be expressed as a general modularity conjecture relating
            = succeed "-functions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat%27s+last+theorem"} Fermat's last theorem
            by Wiles and Taylor can be interpreted as a proof of a special case of the modularity conjecture (for elliptic curves).
          %h2#AbelianMotives Constructions of the abelian category of mixed motives
          %p
            There is no generally accepted construction of a
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            of mixed motives, and its existence remains conjectural. However, there exist candidate and conditional constructions which are useful in practice.
          %p
            Note that “the” abelian category of mixed motives depends on choosing a base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed ")" do
              %em motivic sheaves
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the spectrum of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
          %h3#nori_motives Nori motives
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Madhav+Nori"} Madhav Nori
            has an approach to the theory of motives based on a peculiar kind of Tannakian reconstruction, the so called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nori%27s+Tannakian+theorem"} Nori's Tannakian theorem
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannakian+category"} Tannakian category
            of mixed motives over any sub
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %h3#deligne_motives Deligne motives
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"} Pierre Deligne
            gave a definition of a category of mixed motives over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+fields"} number fields
            as compatible systems of realizations, essentially bundling together all the structure that mixed motives should give rise to. This approach automatically yields a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannakian+category"} Tannakian category
            of mixed motives with all the desired realization functors (Betti,
            = succeed "-adic," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            %a{:href => "#DeligneP1"} Deligne
            for details.
          %h3#as_the_heart_of_a_tstructure_on_the_derived_category_of_mixed_motives As the heart of a t-structure on the derived category of mixed motives
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"} Deligne
            first suggested that it might be easier to define the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S,\mathbb{Q})
            of the hypothetical abelian category of mixed motives. Once this is done, one can in principle recover the abelian category as the heart of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/t-structure"} t-structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DM
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S,\mathbb{Q})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S,\mathbb{Q})
            is over any base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            (
            %a{:href => "#DerivedMotives"}> see below
            ). The hypothetical t-structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S,\mathbb{Q})
            whose heart is the abelian category of mixed motives over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong motivic t-structure
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"} Beilinson
            proved that, over fields of characteristic zero, the existence of the motivic t-structure implies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+conjectures+on+algebraic+cycles"} standard conjectures on algebraic cycles
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Beilinson10"} Beilinson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Bondarko"} Bondarko
            proved that it implies the existence of motivic t-structures for more general schemes (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Bondarko13"} Bondarko
          %p
            While the derived category of mixed motives can also be defined with integral rather than rational coefficients,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Voevodksy
            observed that the derived category of integral motives cannot have a motivic t-structure (
            %a{:href => "#TriCatMixMot"}> Voevodsky, Prop. 4.3.8
            ). Thus, the
            %em abelian
            category of motives always refers to motives with rational coefficients.
          %h3#references References
          %ul#Beilinson10
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"}> Alexander Beilinson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Remarks on Grothendieck’s standard conjectures
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1006.1116"} arXiv
          %ul#Bondarko13
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Bondarko"}> Mikhail Bondarko
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mixed motivic sheaves (and weights for them) exist if ‘ordinary’ mixed motives do
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1105.0420"} arXiv
          %ul#DeligneP1
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Le groupe fondamental de la droite projective moins trois points
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://publications.ias.edu/sites/default/files/61_LeGroupeFondamentalDroite.pdf"} pdf
          %ul#TriCatMixMot
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Triangulated categories of motives over a field
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0074/"} K-theory
          %h2#DerivedMotives Constructions of the derived category of mixed motives
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of the hypothetical abelian category of mixed motives has been unconditionally defined over any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+scheme"} Noetherian scheme
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Voevodksy
            in the mid 1990s. Since then, several other definitions were formulated: one by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fabien+Morel"} Morel
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Ayoub"} Ayoub
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Cisinski
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+D%C3%A9glise"} Déglise
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            %span.newWikiWord
              excellent
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/excellent+scheme"} ?
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              geometrically unibranch
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometrically+unibranch+scheme"} ?
            schemes.
          %p
            On the other hand, Voevodsky’s definition is the only one among these four which also makes sense with integral coefficients rather than rational coefficients. Recently,
            %span.newWikiWord
              Spitzweck
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Markus%2BSpitzweck"} ?
            proposed a definition of the category of integral motives over general base schemes which also supports a formalism of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
            of characteristic zero. Rationally, however, it agrees with the Morel/Ayoub/Cisinski-Déglise definition over any base scheme.
          %h3#VoevodskyMotives As homotopy invariant Nisnevich sheaves with transfers (Voevodsky motives)
          %p
            Associated to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+scheme"} Noetherian scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SmCor
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SmCor_S
            of “finite”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                are smooth schemes of finite type over
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
                s
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi SmCor
                        %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SmCor_S(X,Y)
                are the abelian group of cycles on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi S
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times_S Y
                that are “universally integral relative to
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motive"} pure motive
            for more (see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MaVoWe"} MaVoWe, Appendix 1A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sm
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mi SmCor
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sm/S\hookrightarrow SmCor_S
          %p
            An
            %strong
              (∞,1)-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+with+transfers"} presheaf with transfers
            on the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sm
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sm/S
            of smooth schemes of finite type is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaf"} (∞,1)-presheaf
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SmCor
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SmCor_S
            which transforms finite sums into finite (∞,1)-products (and hence take values in connective
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
          %p
            The (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi DM
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %mi eff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM^{eff}_{\geq 0}(S)
            is a certain reflexive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            of the (∞,1)-category of presheaves with transfers: it consists of those presheaves with transfers whose underlying presheaves on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sm
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sm/S
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nisnevich+topology"} Nisnevich topology
            and are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A1-homotopy+theory"} A1-homotopy invariant
          %p
            The
            %strong Tate motive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}(1)[2]
            is the image of the pointed scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{P}^1,\infty)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi DM
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %mi eff
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM^{eff}_{\geq 0}(S)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The stable (∞,1)-category of (integral) motives
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DM
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi DM
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %mi eff
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM^{eff}_{\geq 0}(S)
              at the Tate motive
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}(1)[2]
          %p
            The (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S)
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable
            (∞,1)-category which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi DM
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM^-(S)
            defined in
            %a{:href => "#MaVoWe"} MaVoWe, p. 110
            which is not cocomplete.
          %p
            %em Voevodsky’s cancellation theorem
            states that the canonical functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi DM
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %mi eff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM^{eff}_{\geq 0}(S)\to DM(S)
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+field"} perfect field
          %h3#as_rational_stable_motivic_homotopy_types_with_trivial_action_of_the_hopf_element_morel_motives As rational stable motivic homotopy types with trivial action of the Hopf element (Morel motives)
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon : \mathbb{G}_m\wedge \mathbb{G}_m\to \mathbb{G}_m\wedge \mathbb{G}_m
            be the transposition. In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+motivic+homotopy+category"} stable motivic homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)
            this becomes an endomorphism of the motivic sphere spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^0
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon^2=1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi ϵ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi ϵ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1+\epsilon}{2}, \quad \frac{1-\epsilon}{2}
          %p
            which induce a splitting
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℚ
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mo +
                    %mo ×
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}}\simeq SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}_+}\times SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}_-}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}_+}
              is the stable
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "." do
                %em Morel motives
          %p
            In other words, a Morel motive is a rational stable motivic homotopy type on which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            acts as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
          %p
            The
            %em Hopf element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\in \pi_{1,1}(S^0)
            is the stabilization of the algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibration"} Hopf fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^2-0\to\mathbb{P}^1
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            We have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon=-1
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            is a sum of squares in all the residue fields of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℚ
                    %mo =
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}}= SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}_+}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}_-}
            only appears over
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+real+field"} formally real field
            = succeed "." do
              %em Witt motives
          %h3#as_homotopy_invariant_tale_sheaves_without_transfers_ayoub_motives As homotopy invariant étale sheaves without transfers (Ayoub motives)
          %p
            According to Ayoub, the stable
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            can be constructed in the same way as the stable motivic homotopy category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)
          %ul
            %li
              Instead of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nisnevich+topology"} Nisnevich topology
              on the category of smooth
              = succeed "-schemes," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+topology"} étale topology
            %li
              Instead of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
              of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          %p
            The resulting (∞,1)-category is denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi DA
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} et
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DA^{\mathrm{et}}(S,\mathbb{Q})
            = succeed "-spectra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1
            = succeed "-invariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
            with values in connective rational
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
          %h3#as_modules_over_a_summand_of_rational_homotopy_invariant_algebraic_ktheory_beilinson_motives As modules over a summand of rational homotopy invariant algebraic K-theory (Beilinson motives)
          %p
            This definition is due to Cisinski and Déglise. The rationalization of the homotopy invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KGL
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi SH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KGL\in SH(S)
            splits as a direct sum
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi KGL
                    %mi ℚ
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KGL_{\mathbb{Q}} = \bigoplus_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} \Sigma_T^n H_B
          %p
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            rational motivic ring spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_B\in SH(S)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The stable
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %em Beilinson motives
              is the
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)_{\mathbb{Q}}
              consisting of
              = succeed "-local" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_B
          %p
            Cisinski and Déglise have shown that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_B
            is exactly the
            = succeed "-summand" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mo +
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^0_{\mathbb{Q}_+}
            of the rational motivic sphere spectrum, and hence that a Beilinson motive is the same thing as a Morel motive. They have also shown that Beilinson/Morel motives are equivalent to Ayoub motives. Finally, they have shown that Beilinson motives are equivalent to rational Voevodsky motives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(S,\mathbb{Q})
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is
            %span.newWikiWord
              excellent
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/excellent+scheme"} ?
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                geometrically unibranch
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometrically+unibranch+scheme"} ?
          %h3#as_modules_over_a_spectrum_representing_motivic_cohomology_over__spitzweck_motives
            As modules over a spectrum representing motivic cohomology over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec \mathbb{Z}
            (Spitzweck motives)
          %p
            One idea to define a category of integral motives with a formalism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            is to first define an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            motivic ring spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi ℤ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{\mathbb{Z}}\in SH(Spec \mathbb{Z})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: S\to Spec \mathbb{Z}
            is any scheme, we obtain an
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_S = f^\ast(M_{\mathbb{Z}})
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_S
            for varying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            then inherit a complete formalism of six operations from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markus+Spitzweck"} Spitzweck
            defined such an
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{\mathbb{Z}}
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is smooth over a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+domain"} Dedekind domain
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_S
              represents
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} Bloch-Levine motivic cohomology
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is smooth over a field,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_S
              is equivalent to Voevodsky’s motivic Eilenberg-Mac Lane spectrum
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\mathbb{Z}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi ℤ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{Q}
              is the Beilinson motive
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_B
          %p
            The stable
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a field of characteristic zero (by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#RO08"} Rondigs-Ostvaer, Theorem 5.5
          %h2#variations_and_extensions Variations and extensions
          %p
            Correspondences are interesting in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            flavour. For example, KK-groups are in fact themselves sort of correspondences;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Connes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Skandalis"} Skandalis
            had an early reference very much paralleling some ideas from the algebraic world. More recently, motives in the operator algebraic setup have been approached by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Connes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Marcolli
            and others.
          %p#Noncommutative
            In derived
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            based on
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            proposed a theory of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motive"} noncommutative motive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Cisinski
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"} Tabuada
            %a{:href => "noncommutative+motive#References"} references
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motive"} noncommutative motive
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/birational+geometry"} birational geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Kahn"} Bruno Kahn
            defined the appropriate version. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^1
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^1
          %h2#relation_to_other_fields Relation to other fields
          %h3#relation_to_physics Relation to physics
          %p
            Motivic structures show up in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+multiple+zeta+values"} motivic multiple zeta values
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"} pull-push quantization
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten theory
            is naturally understood as a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"}> motivic quantization
            ” in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+motives"} Chow motives
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stacks"} Deligne-Mumford stacks
            (
            %a{:href => "#BehrendManin95"}> Behrend-Manin 95
            ).
          %h3#relation_to_kktheory Relation to KK-theory
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "KK-theory#AsAnAnalogOfMotives"} As an analog of motives in noncommutative topology
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motive"}> pure motive
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+motive"} mixed motive
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Voevodsky+motive"} Voevodsky motive
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nori+motive"} Nori motive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+motive"} Chow motive
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-motive"}> K-motive
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motive"} noncommutative motive
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+with+transfer"} presheaf with transfer
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+theory"} motivic homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function"}> zeta function
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+multiple+zeta+values"} motivic multiple zeta values
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+function"} motivic function
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Donaldson-Thomas+invariant"} motivic Donaldson-Thomas invariant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transform"} Fourier-Mukai transform
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berkovich+motive"} Berkovich motive
            %li
              %p
                See also at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "KK-theory#AsAnAnalogOfMotives"} KK-theory – Relation to motives
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives+in+physics"} motives in physics
          %h2#references_2 References
          %h3#general General
          %p A brief exposition is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barry+Mazur"}> Barry Mazur
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em What is… a Motive?
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/200410/what-is.pdf"} pdf
          %p A review is also in chapter I of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noncommutative+Geometry%2C+Quantum+Fields+and+Motives"} Noncommutative Geometry, Quantum Fields and Motives
          %p Lectures include
          %ul
            %li#Levine06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Levine"}> Marc Levine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Six lectures on motives
              %em ICTP Workshop on K-theory and Motives
              2006 (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LevineMotiveLecture.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The definition of Voevodsky motives can be found in
          %ul#MaVoWe
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlo+Mazza"}> Carlo Mazza
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Weibel"} Charles Weibel
              %em Lectures in motivic cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/motiviclectures.html"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.claymath.org/library/monographs/cmim02.pdf"} pdf
          %p Voevodsky’s formalization of motives was sketched in
          %ul#Deligne
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
              ,
              %em Voevodsky lectures on cross functors,
              %p
                Fall 2001. (
                %a{:href => "http://mat.uab.cat/~kock/tmp/delnotes01.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p and worked out in detail in
          %ul#Ayoub
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Ayoub"}> Joseph Ayoub
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Les six opérations de Grothendieck et le formalisme des cycles évanescents dans le monde motivique (I)
              %p
                %em> Les six opérations de Grothendieck et le formalisme des cycles évanescents dans le monde motivique (II)
                , Astérisque, vol. 315, Soc. Math. France, 2007.
          %p the definition of Ayoub motives in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Ayoub"}> Joseph Ayoub
              ,
              %em La réalisation étale et les opérations de Grothendieck
              (
              %a{:href => "http://user.math.uzh.ch/ayoub/PDF-Files/Realisation-Etale.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p For the definition of Beilinson and Morel motives, the equivalences of the various definitions, and the formalism of six operations, see
          %ul#CD12
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+D%C3%A9glise"} Frédéric Déglise
              %em Triangulated categories of mixed motives
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.2110"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            The fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DM(k)
            is equivalent to the category of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(\mathbb{Z})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} char
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{char}(k)=0
            is proved in
          %ul#RO08
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oliver+R%C3%B6ndigs"}> Oliver Röndigs
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Arne+%C3%98stv%C3%A6r"} Paul Arne Østvær
              %em Modules over motivic cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/~oroendig/MZfinal.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Spitzweck’s definition of a motivic cohomology spectrum over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec \mathbb{Z}
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markus+Spitzweck"}> Markus Spitzweck
              ,
              %em A commutative P^1-spectrum representing motivic cohomology over Dedekind domains I
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.4078"}> arXiv
              )
          %p A summary of the axioms and of the main theorems is in the introduction of
          %ul#CisinskiDeglise
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+D%C3%A9glise"} Frédéric Déglise
              = succeed "," do
                %em Triangulated categories of mixed motives
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.2110"} arxiv/0912.2110
          %p An outline of the big picture can be found in the introduction to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Kaledin"}> Dmitri Kaledin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Motivic structures in non-commutative geometry
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3210"} arxiv/1003.3210
          %p Some pretty useful comments on motives are at this MathOverflow thead:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/2146/whats-the-yoga-of-motives/2230#2230"} What’s the “Yoga of Motives?”
          %p See also the blog post
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Speyer"}> David Speyer
              ,
              %em Motive-ating the Weil conjecture proof
              (
              %a{:href => "http://sbseminar.wordpress.com/2010/06/10/motive-ating-the-weil-conjecture-proof/"}> blog
              )
          %p
            A formal discussion of motives can be found in
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/MVWnotes/prova-hyperlink.pdf#page=121"} lecture 14
            of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlo+Mazza"}> Carlo Mazza
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Weibel"} Charles Weibel
              %em Lectures in motivic cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/motiviclectures.html"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/MVWnotes/prova-hyperlink.pdf"} pdf
          %p There is also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Milne"}> James S. Milne
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.jmilne.org/math/xnotes/MOT.pdf"} Motives – Grothendieck’s Dream
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minhyong+Kim"}> Minhyong Kim
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ucl.ac.uk/~ucahmki/ihes3.pdf"}
                    Classical Motives: Motivic
                    = succeed "-functions" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi L
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Kahn"}> Bruno Kahn
                ,
                %a{:href => "http://www.aimath.org/WWN/motivesdessins/PaloAlto1.pdf"} pdf slides
                on pure motives
            %li
              %p
                Florence Lecomte, Nathalie Wach,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Réalisations des complexes motiviques de Voevodsky
                %a{:href => "http://de.arxiv.org/abs/0911.5611"} arxiv:0911.5611
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Levine"}> Marc Levine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Smooth motives
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0807.2265"} arxiv:0807.2265
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Levine"}> Marc Levine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mixed motives
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 57
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/online_bks/surv57/surv57.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For a noncommutative analogue to the theory of motives, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
          %p Some other aspects
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Bondarko"}> Mikhail V. Bondarko
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Weight structures vs.
                    = succeed "-structures;" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi t
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0704.4003"} arxiv/0704.4003
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"}> Yuri Manin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Motives and quantum cohomology
                %a{:href => "http://www.dailymotion.com/video/x8juco_colloque-grothendieck-yuri-manin_tech"} video
          %p Motives from the point of view of Grothendieck topoi are studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivia+Caramello"}> Olivia Caramello
              ,
              %em
                Motivic toposes,
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1507.06271"} arxiv/1507.06271
          %h3#relation_to_hodge_theory Relation to Hodge theory
          %p
            Explicit discussion of the relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Peters"}> Chris Peters
              ,
              %em Tata Lectures on Motivic Aspects of Hodge Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www-fourier.ujf-grenoble.fr/~peters/motivic.f/Tatalects.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h3#RelationToKKTheory Relation KK-theory and bivariant K-theory, K-motives
          %p
            Relation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bivariant+algebraic+K-theory"} bivariant algebraic K-theory
            (see also at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
          %ul#ConnesConsaniMarcolli05
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Caterina+Consani"} Caterina Consani
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em Noncommutative geometry and motives: the thermodynamics of endomotives
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512138"}> arXiv:math/0512138
              )
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guillermo+Corti%C3%B1as"}> Guillermo Cortiñas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Thom"} Andreas Thom
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Bivariant algebraic K-theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0603531"} arXiv:math/0603531
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigory+Garkusha"}> Grigory Garkusha
                , Ivan Panin,
                %em K-motives of algebraic varieties
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1108.0375"}> arXiv:1108.0375
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigory+Garkusha"}> Grigory Garkusha
                ,
                %em Algebraic Kasparov K-theory. II
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1206.0178"}> arXiv:1206.0178
                )
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/KK-theory#AsAnAnalogOfMotives"} KK-theory – Relation to motives
          %p For a collection of literature see also paragraph 1.5 in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Blumberg"}> Andrew Blumberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Gepner"} David Gepner
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"} Gonçalo Tabuada
              %em A universal characterization of higher algebraic K-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.2282"}> arXiv:1001.2282
              )
          %p
            (in the context of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
          %h3#in_physics In physics
          %ul
            %li
              A. Rej,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em Motives, an introductory survey for physicists
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.its.caltech.edu/~matilde/ObiMotivesSurveyFinal.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+multiple+zeta+values"} motivic multiple zeta values
          %p
            That the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"} pull-push quantization
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten theory
            is naturally understood as a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"}> motivic quantization
            ” in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+motives"} Chow motives
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stacks"} Deligne-Mumford stacks
            was suggested in
          %ul#BehrendManin95
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Behrend"}> Kai Behrend
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"} Yuri Manin
              %em Stacks of Stable Maps and Gromov-Witten Invariants
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/alg-geom/9506023"}> arXiv:alg-geom/9506023
              )
          %p Further investigation of these stacky Chow motives then appears in
          %ul#Toen00
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"}> Bertrand Toën
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On motives for Deligne-Mumford stacks
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0006160"} arXiv:math/0006160
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00773027"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/docs/00/77/30/27/PDF/motdm.pdf"} pdf
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Utsav+Choudhury"}> Utsav Choudhury
              ,
              %em Motives of Deligne-Mumford Stacks
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1109.5288"}> arXiv:1109.5288
              )
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives+in+physics"} motives in physics
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
